=== REASONING ===
Implementation strategy (one decision per contract rule from
predecessor's REASONING block):

  D1 (R1 live wins). Phase 1 reads the live store first and writes
      every available value into `state`. Phase 2 (the new fallback)
      checks `state.<field> == zero` before writing — never overwrites
      a live value. For booleans where Go's zero (false) is
      indistinguishable from "unset", the fallback inspects the raw
      live signal map (`live[<signal>]` presence) instead of the state
      field, avoiding the false-vs-unset ambiguity that would let a
      stale signal_log `true` clobber a real live `false`.

  D2 (R2 ADR-001). Only `s.signalLogReader.SnapshotAt` is called. No
      query against positions / state_snapshots / battery_snapshots /
      climate_snapshots / security_snapshots / tire_pressure_snapshots
      / location_snapshots / safety_snapshots /
      vehicle_config_snapshots / user_preference_snapshots / media_state
      / vehicle_live_state. Gate's NO_SNAPSHOT_TABLE_READS anchor
      enforces this on every CI run.

  D3 (R3 missing-signal tolerance). Each fallback assignment guards
      with `if v, ok := snap[<key>]; ok { ... }` via the snapFloat /
      snapString / snapBool helpers. A nil or absent map value leaves
      the corresponding state field at its Go zero value with no
      panic. Test T3 locks this down.

  D4 (R4 read-error tolerance). On `SnapshotAt` returning a non-nil
      error, the implementation logs at `log.Warn().Err(err)…` and
      then continues. The function does not return an error (its
      signature has no error return) and the live-only state still
      flows out. Test T4 captures the zerolog buffer and asserts both
      the warn level and the sentinel error message landed there.

  D5 (R5 nil-reader safety). `BuildStateFromSignalStore` checks
      `if s.signalLogReader != nil` before calling `SnapshotAt`. The
      stateRepo Phase-3 fallback gets the same nil guard so legacy /
      test callers that wire neither survive without nil-pointer
      panics. Test T5 verifies the legacy path.

  D6 (R6 mapping parity). The fallback uses the exact field-to-signal
      mapping enumerated in the predecessor matrix:
        Odometer        ← Odometer
        InsideTemp      ← InsideTemp
        OutsideTemp     ← OutsideTemp
        SoftwareVersion ← Version, then SoftwareUpdateVersion
        IdealRange      ← IdealBatteryRange
        RatedRange      ← RatedRange, then EstBatteryRange
        Latitude        ← Latitude
        Longitude       ← Longitude
        BatteryLevel    ← BatteryLevel, then Soc
        Speed           ← VehicleSpeed
        IsLocked        ← Locked        (live-presence-gated)
        SentryMode      ← SentryMode    (live-presence-gated)
        IsClimateOn     ← HvacPower     (live-presence-gated)

  D7 (test scope). Tests live in
      internal/service/vehicle_service_test.go (package service —
      internal tests so the fakeSnapshotReader can satisfy the
      service.SignalSnapshotReader interface and so VehicleService
      fields stay accessible). VehicleService is constructed manually
      via `&VehicleService{}` + `WithSignalLogReader(fake)` so no real
      *database.DB is needed; the nil stateRepo guard added by D5 is
      what enables this.

Wiring decision: the production *database.SignalLogReader satisfies
service.SignalSnapshotReader implicitly (it already has the exact
SnapshotAt signature). router.go threads the same instance into both
the existing handler wiring AND the service via the new
`vehicleSvc.WithSignalLogReader(signalLogReader)` call — no second
NewSignalLogReader allocation, no double-pool overhead.

Handler wire decision: vehicle_handler.go is intentionally NOT
modified. The CurrentState handler already calls
`h.vehicleSvc.BuildStateFromSignalStore(...)` and writes 200 on the
returned non-nil state. Because the new fallback never returns an
error (D4), the handler continues to 200 on signal_log read failure
exactly as the prompt requires.

=== CHANGES ===
1. internal/service/vehicle_service.go
   - Added `time` import.
   - Added exported interface `SignalSnapshotReader { SnapshotAt(ctx,
     vehicleID, at) (map[string]interface{}, error) }` with the
     canonical doc-comment phrase
     "interface { ... SnapshotAt(...) }" so the gate's line-based
     READER_INTERFACE_DEFINED anchor matches.
   - Added unexported field `signalLogReader SignalSnapshotReader` to
     VehicleService.
   - Added builder `WithSignalLogReader(reader) *VehicleService`.
   - In `BuildStateFromSignalStore`:
       * Inserted Phase 2 between the existing Phase 1 (live store
         reads) and the legacy stateRepo fallback. Calls
         `s.signalLogReader.SnapshotAt(ctx, vehicle.ID,
         time.Now().UTC())` exactly once when the reader is wired,
         logs Warn on error, never propagates.
       * Delegates per-field fill to new helper `fillStateFromSnapshot`
         which only writes to fields still at the Go zero value (R1)
         using `snapFloat` / `snapString` / `snapBool` helpers. Booleans
         (Locked / SentryMode / HvacPower) are gated on
         `_, present := live[<signal>]; !present` to dodge the
         false-vs-unset ambiguity.
       * Wrapped the existing `s.stateRepo.GetCurrentState` call with
         `s.stateRepo != nil` so test callers can construct a
         VehicleService without a real *database.DB.

